<section xml:id="section-derivative-rules-proofs">
  <title>SLO 1: Proofs Rules for Differentiation</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <xref ref="section-derivative-rules" /> and 
      <xref ref="section-chain-rule" /> introduce the main derivative
      rules. The rules in that section were presented as truth, but
      without argument. In mathematics, truth is established by proof.
      If these rules are treated as mathematically true, they must be
      proven. In this section, I'm going to go over the proofs for the
      main rule. 
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-proof-sinusoidal-derivative">
    <title>Derivatives of Sinusoidal Functions</title>
    <p>
      I said in <xref ref="section-derivative-rules" /> that the
      follwing derivatives were true. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
        \frac{d}{dx} \sin x \amp = \cos x \amp \frac{d}{dx} \cos
        x \amp = -\sin x
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Why are these true? Well, let me use the limit definition of the
      derivative to prove the first of these. 
      <md>
        \frac{d}{dx} \sin x = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{\sin (x +
        h) - \sin x}{h}
      </md>
      The difficult here is the sum inside the sine function.
      Thankfuilly, there is a trig identity that cover this case. I'll
      use the identity for sine applied to a sum. 
      <md>
        = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{ \sin x \cos h + \cos x \sin h
        - \sin x}{h} 
      </md>
      Then I can split up this limit and group it into two. 
      <md>
        = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{ \sin x \cos h - \sin x}{h} 
        + \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{ \cos x \sin h }{h} 
      </md>
      Then I factor and pull anything out of the limit that doesn't
      involve the limit variable <m>h</m>. 
      <md>
        = \sin x \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{ \cos h - 1}{h} 
        + \cos x \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{ \sin h }{h} 
      </md>
      Both of these are exactly the special trig limit fromn <xref
      ref="subsection-three-special-limits"/>. I can just use those
      known limits. 
      <md>
        = \sin x (0) + \cos x (1) = \cos x 
      </md>
      This completes the argument. The proof for cosine is very
      similar. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-proof-exponential-derivative">
    <title>Derivatives of Exponential Functions</title>
    <p>
      This is the result that I stated for the derivative of an
      exponential funciton. 
      <md>
        \frac{d}{dx} a^x = a^x \ln a 
      </md>
      Why is this true? I'll start with the second version of the
      definition of the derivative and do some algebra with the limit.
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \frac{d}{dx} a^x \amp = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0}
          \frac{a^{x+h} - a^x}{h} = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{a^x
          a^h - a^x}{h}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} a^x \frac{a^h-1}{h} = a^x
          \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{a^h-1}{h} = a^x \left( \left.
          \frac{d}{dx} a^x \right|_{x=0} \right)
        </mrow>
      </md>
      In the last step, I changed the limit into a derivative using
      the definition of the derivative at the specific point
      <m>x=0</m>. The vertical line means <q>evaluated at
      <m>x=0</m></q>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This formula shows that derivative of <m>a^x</m> is a product of
      the same function <m>a^x</m> multiplied by its derivative at
      <m>x=0</m>. Note that all exponentials go through the point
      <m>(0,1)</m>. <xref ref="figure-four-exponential-functions" />
      shows the the graphs of several exponential function (for bases
      <m>a > 1</m>).
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-four-exponential-functions">
      <caption>Four Exponential Functions</caption>
      <image xml:id="figure49" width="60%">
        <asymptote>
          size(10cm,5cm,IgnoreAspect);

          import graph;

          xaxis("$x$");
          yaxis("$y$");

          real f1(real x) {return 2^x;}
          real f2(real x) {return 3^x;}
          real f4(real x) {return 5^x;}
          real f3(real x) {return 4^x;}

          draw(graph(f1,-3,1));
          draw(graph(f2,-3,1));
          draw(graph(f3,-3,1));
          draw(graph(f4,-3,1));

          label("$2^x$",(1,2),E);
          label("$3^x$",(1,3),E);
          label("$4^x$",(1,4),E);
          label("$5^x$",(1,5),E);
        </asymptote>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The slope at <m>(0,1)</m> determines the derivative. As seen in
      <xref ref="figure-four-exponential-functions" />, for different
      choices of the base, there are different slopes of the graph
      when it passes through the point <m>(0,1)</m>. There is one
      special base which has slope <m>1</m> at the point <m>(0,1)</m>.
      That base is the number <m>e</m>. (As I argued before, you can
      take this as the definition of this number, if you wish.) 
    </p>
    <p>
      Once I identify <m>e</m> as the special number where the
      exponential with base <m>e</m> has slope, I can actually
      calculate what this constant in the derivative works out to be.
      This is a chain rule calculation using solve clever tricks with
      exponent laws to right any exponential as a base <m>e</m>
      exponential. The inside function for the chain rule is <m>x \ln
      a</m>. 
    </p>
    <md>
      \frac{d}{dx} a^x = \frac{d}{dx e^{ln a^x} = \frac{d}{dx} e^{x \ln
      a} = e^{x \ln a} \frac{d}{dx} x \ln a = a^x \ln a 
    </md>
    <p>
      This completes the proof. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-power-rule-proof">
    <title>Power Rule</title>
    <p>
      Recall the power rule now. 
      <md>
        \frac{d}{dx} x^n = nx^{n-1}
      </md>
      I can give a proof when <m>n</m> is a positive integer, using
      the binomial theorem. (The power rule applies for all real
      exponents, but the general proof is less accessible.) 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \frac{d}{dx} x^n \amp = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0}
          \frac{(x+h)^n - x^n}{h} =
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{x^n + nx^{n-1}h +
          \binom{n}{2} x^{n-2}h^2 + \ldots + nxh^{n-1} + h^n -
          x^n}{h}
        </mrow>
      </md>
      I've expended the binomial in the numerator by the binomial
      theorem. After doing this, the first term of the expansion will
      always cancel with the last term in the numerator. The resulting
      terms all have <m>h</m> in them, so I can factor that <m>h</m>
      out, cancel it off, and then evaluate the limit.
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \frac{d}{dx} x^n \amp = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{nx^{n-1}h +
          \binom{n}{2} x^{n-2}h^2 + \ldots + nxh^{n-1} + h^n}{h}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} nx^{n-1} + \binom{n}{2}
          x^{n-2}h + \ldots + nxh^{n-2} + h^{n-1}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = nx^{n-1}
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-derivatives-linearity-proof">
    <title>Linearity</title>
    <p>
      These are the three parts of linearity. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \frac{d}{dx} f + g \amp = \frac{df}{dx} + \frac{dg}{dx}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \frac{d}{dx} f - g \amp = \frac{df}{dx} - \frac{dg}{dx}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \frac{d}{dx} cf \amp = c \frac{df}{dx}
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      I'll prove the first of these. The proof of linearity is
      basically just borrowing from the linearity property of limits.
      Here is the limit definition of the derivative applied to a sum. 
      <md>
        \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(x + h) + g(x + h) - (f(x) +
        g(x))}{h}
      </md>
      I just group this number into the two appropriate piece and
      split up the fraction and the limit. 
      <md>
        = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(x + h) - f(x)}{h}
        + \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{g(x + h) - g(x)}{h} =
        \frac{df}{dx} + \frac{dg}{dx} 
      </md>
      That completes the proof. The other two identities are proved
      similarly. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-leibniz-rule-proof">
    <title>Leibniz Rule</title>
    <p>
      Here is the formal statement of the Leibniz rule and its proof. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> be differentiable functions.
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx} fg = g \frac{df}{dx} + f \frac{dg}{dx}
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I start with the limit definition of the derivative, using
          the version with <m>h \rightarrow 0</m>.
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx} fg = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0}
            \frac{f(x+h)g(x+h) - f(x)g(x)}{h}
          </md>
          Then I'm going to use a strange but surprisingly common
          algebraic trick. I want to do some particular kind of
          factoring in the numerator. To make that work, I add and
          subtract the same term from the numerator. Adding and
          subtracting the same thing doesn't change the expression, so
          equality is preserved. 
          <md>
            = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(x+h)g(x+h) - f(x+h)
            g(x) + f(x+h) g(x) - f(x)g(x)}{h}
          </md>
          Then I'm going to write the first two terms as one limit and
          the next two terms as another limit. In the first two terms,
          <m>f(x+h)</m> is a common factor, so I can pull it out of
          the fraction. In the second two terms, <m>g(x)</m> is a
          common factor, so I can likewise pull it out of the
          fraction.
          <md>
            = \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} f(x+h)\frac{g(x+h) - g(x)}{h} +
            \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} g(x) \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}
          </md>
          I can use the limit rules for the first limit. The limit of
          <m>f(x+h)</m> as <m>h \rightarrow 0</m> is just <m>f(x)</m>,
          so I'll evaluate that piece of the product and leave the
          remaining limit. In the second limit, <m>g(x)</m> doesn't
          involve the variable <m>h</m> at all, so it can simply be
          pulled out of the limitl.
          <md>
            = f(x) \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{g(x+h) - g(x)}{h} +
            g(x) \lim_{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}
          </md>
          Finally, I can simplify identify these two limits as the
          definitions of the derivatives of <m>g(x)</m> and
          <m>f(x)</m>, respectively. 
          <md>
            = f(x) \frac{dg}{dx} + g(x) \frac{df}{dx}
          </md>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </subsection>
</section>
